      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly! During the 20th century, labor unions played a pivotal role in improving working conditions and advocating for fair wages, which helped establish a robust middle class. Through collective bargaining, unions secured better pay, benefits, and job security for their members, which in turn led to increased consumer spending and economic growth. This growth not only elevated the quality of life for many Americans but also contributed to greater social mobility. Furthermore, by giving workers a collective voice, unions promoted democratic principles and influenced public policy, ensuring that the interests of workers were represented at the political level. Thus, unionization helped to build a strong middle class and reinforced democratic values in the United States.",
